\section{Compile a block in the context of its incoming edge}

Conventional flow analysis merges facts at control-flow joins. If a local can be either a small integer or an object, a single compiled join block may need a type test even though each predecessor knows its incoming value's tag. In \emph{Simple and Effective Type Check Removal through Lazy Basic Block Versioning}, Chevalier-Boisvert and Feeley give the join multiple implementations. Each implementation has a context of facts about its live SSA values. A compilation stub on an unexplored edge can request a version when execution first takes that edge. As the compiler walks the successor, it propagates facts and removes redundant tests. The compiled block still has all its reachable outcomes; the selection unit is a block and an entry context, not an entire recorded path as in Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-253}.

Facts may come from a branch just executed, a constant-producing instruction or a proven callee contract. A profile can suggest which edge to compile first, but cannot by itself establish a fact. In particular, a block version entered solely from a successful test need not deoptimize if the test is false: the failing edge already has its own legal continuation.

\section{Implementing a bounded version table}

This example evaluates \texttt{Inc} on the value supplied by either arm. It increments exact integers and uses a generic operation for all other values, including its defined error cases. The taken edge carries a fact about the SSA operand handed to \texttt{Join}, not about every SSA value that ever used its physical register.

\begin{verbatim}
Test(a, b):
  if is_small_int(a): goto Join(a) with x:SmallInt
  else:              goto Join(b) with no fact
Join(x):
  return Inc(x)

Join[SmallInt](x: tagged):
  z, overflow = add_checked(unbox(x), 1)
  if overflow: return IncGeneric(x)
  return box_small(z)
Join[Generic](x: tagged):
  return IncGeneric(x)

version(block, edge_facts):
  facts = project_onto_live_block_parameters(edge_facts)
  exact = lookup_version(block, facts)
  if exact != none: return exact
  if facts and num_specialized(block) < cap:
    return compile_and_register(block, facts)
  return most_precise_compatible_or_generic(block, facts)
\end{verbatim}

An existing version is compatible only if the edge facts imply every entry assumption of the version. In the simple set representation this is a subset test in the direction \emph{version requirements are a subset of edge facts}. With one specialized slot, a future edge with a known object tag can use \texttt{Join[Generic]}, never \texttt{Join[SmallInt]}. Both versions in the example take tagged values; if a backend uses distinct unboxed entry layouts, it must also generate the required edge adapter. A real emitter reserves a version slot before generating nested blocks, or defers linking through stubs, so a self-edge does not recursively compile the same key.

Test \texttt{a=4} for 5 and use a non-integer \texttt{a} with a varying \texttt{b} to exercise the generic version independently. Force cap zero and cap one, and compare return values and exceptions. Test maximal small-integer \texttt{a} separately: its tag proves that unboxing is safe, not that addition cannot overflow. In compiler dumps, assert that every patched edge both entails the target context and implements the parameter mapping.

\section{Heap facts, production scale and version budgets}

Chevalier-Boisvert and Feeley's \emph{Extending Basic Block Versioning with Typed Object Shapes} associates type information with property layouts so a checked shape can justify both the offset and type of a load. Shape facts are not immutable-value facts. An unknown call or a store through an alias can change the shape: discard the dependent fact, or use a proof or a renewed guard. In \emph{YJIT: A Basic Block Versioning JIT Compiler for CRuby}, Chevalier-Boisvert and colleagues describe an implementation that compiles lazily and can return to the interpreter by restoring its PC and stack pointer. This is not a contradiction of non-speculative edge reasoning: an implementation can combine proved context facts with speculative fast paths whose misses have separate exits.

Dossier Chapter~20 treats variants as persistent siblings, and Chapter~41 proposes choosing optimization granularity adaptively. BBV supplies an established block-sized design point, not the adaptive policy. In the blueprint's terms, the exploration plane could rank block contexts under a byte budget, but assurance still checks incoming facts and exit maps. Benchmark generic-only versus capped BBV while counting distinct contexts, stub hits, removed type tests and total code bytes. A program that cycles through many unrelated heap shapes tests whether the generic fallback bounds cost more effectively than compiling another plausible-looking specialization.
